{
  "generated_text": "To determine the number of shirt-and-tie outfits that can be made without wearing a shirt and a tie of the same color, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the total number of shirts and ties available:**\n   - There are 8 colors.\n   - For each color, there is one shirt and one tie.\n   - Therefore, there are 8 shirts and 8 ties.\n\n2. **Calculate the total number of possible shirt-and-tie combinations without any restrictions:**\n   - Each shirt can be paired with any of the 8 ties.\n   - Therefore, the total number of possible shirt-and-tie combinations is:\n     \\[\n     8 \\text{ shirts} \\times 8 \\text{ ties} = 64 \\text{ combinations}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Determine the number of combinations where the shirt and tie are of the same color:**\n   - There are 8 colors, and for each color, there is exactly one shirt and one tie.\n   - Thus, there are 8 combinations where the shirt and tie are of the same color (one for each color).\n\n4. **Calculate the number of valid shirt-and-tie outfits:**\n   - Subtract the number of combinations where the shirt and tie are of the same color from the total number of combinations:\n     \\[\n     64 \\text{ total combinations} - 8 \\text{ same-color combinations} = 56 \\text{ valid combinations}\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of shirt-and-tie outfits that can be made without wearing a shirt and a tie of the same color is \\(\\boxed{56}\\).",
